     "heading": "The pH Scale: Measuring Acidity Precisely",
     "para": "Colour indicators tell us whether a solution is acidic or basic, but to know how acidic or basic it is we use the pH scale, running from 0 to 14. pH means the power of hydrogen: it measures the concentration of H+ ions, but inversely, so the lower the pH, the greater the acidity. A neutral solution like pure water has pH 7. Solutions of pH less than 7 are acidic, and the smaller the number, the more acidic; solutions of pH more than 7 are basic, and the larger the number, the more basic. pH is measured with universal indicator paper, which shows a different colour for each pH value on the chart. This single number connects chemistry to daily life: blood is close to pH 7.4, lemon juice about 2.2, and a change of even one unit means a tenfold change in acidity, which is why small pH shifts matter so much to living things.",
