     "answer": "1) When electricity is passed through brine, an aqueous solution of sodium chloride, it decomposes to give sodium hydroxide, chlorine and hydrogen; since the products are an alkali and chlorine, the process is called the chlor-alkali process. 2) Equation: 2NaCl + 2H2O -> 2NaOH + Cl2 + H2. Chlorine gas is liberated at the anode, hydrogen gas at the cathode, and sodium hydroxide is formed in the solution. 3) Uses of sodium hydroxide: manufacture of soaps and detergents, and degreasing of metals; it is also used in paper making. 4) Uses of chlorine: disinfecting drinking water and swimming pools, and manufacture of bleaching powder, PVC and insecticides. 5) Uses of hydrogen: as a clean fuel, for hydrogenation of vegetable oils into ghee, and for making ammonia which is used in fertilisers. Chlorine and hydrogen can also be combined to make hydrochloric acid used in medicines and cosmetics. The equation, electrode positions and uses carry the marks."
