     "para": "Ionic compounds share a distinct set of properties that follow from their structure of strong electrostatic forces between ions. They are generally hard solids with high melting and boiling points, because large amounts of energy are needed to break the strong electrostatic attraction between the oppositely charged ions packed in the crystal lattice. Sodium chloride melts at about 1074 K and magnesium oxide at about 2852 K. They are generally soluble in water but insoluble in organic solvents like kerosene and petrol, because water molecules can surround and stabilise the ions. Most importantly, they conduct electricity in the molten state and in aqueous solution, because the ions become free to move and carry current; in the solid state the ions are held fixed in the lattice, so solids do not conduct. This is why molten NaCl or brine conducts while dry solid NaCl does not, a difference frequently tested in examinations.",
