     "heading": "Occurrence and Extraction: Three Zones of the Series",
     "para": "Metals occur in nature either in the free native state, like gold, silver and platinum at the bottom of the series, or as compounds called minerals. A mineral from which a metal can be profitably extracted is an ore, and the unwanted earthly impurities mixed with ore form the gangue. The extraction route depends on where the metal sits in the reactivity series. Highly reactive metals like Na, K, Ca, Mg and Al, which form very stable oxides and cannot be reduced by carbon, are extracted by electrolytic reduction of their molten chlorides or oxides, e.g. molten NaCl gives Na at cathode and Cl2 at anode. Moderately reactive metals like Zn, Fe, Pb and Cu occur as sulphides or carbonates and are first converted to oxides by roasting or calcination, then reduced by carbon. Least reactive metals like Hg occur as sulphides and are obtained by heating alone, as with cinnabar 2HgS + 3O2 -> 2HgO + 2SO2 followed by 2HgO -> 2Hg + O2.",
