     "heading": "Refining, Corrosion Prevention and Alloys",
     "para": "The metal obtained by any extraction method still contains impurities and is refined, most commonly by electrolytic refining. In the refining of copper, the impure copper block is the anode, a thin strip of pure copper is the cathode, and copper sulphate solution with a little acid is the electrolyte. On passing current, copper from the anode dissolves as Cu2+ ions and pure copper deposits on the cathode, while insoluble impurities settle below the anode as anode mud, which may contain precious metals like gold and silver. Corrosion of the refined metal is then prevented by painting, oiling, greasing, galvanisation, chrome plating, anodising or alloying. Galvanisation, coating steel with zinc, is used for water pipes and coolers; even if the zinc layer is scratched, zinc corrodes first and protects the iron because zinc is more reactive. Finally, pure metals are often too soft, so alloys are made: steel with carbon, stainless steel with chromium and nickel, brass Cu-Zn, bronze Cu-Sn, solder Pb-Sn and amalgam with mercury.",
