     "question": "Explain the process of fertilization in flowering plants.",
     "marks": 3,
     "type": "short",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "Fertilization in flowering plants is the process where male and female gametes fuse to form a zygote. It occurs after pollination, when pollen grains are transferred from the anther to the stigma of a flower. The pollen grain germinates on the stigma, forming a pollen tube that grows down through the style to the ovary. The generative cell in the pollen grain divides to form two male gametes (sperm cells). The pollen tube enters an ovule in the ovary through the micropyle. One male gamete fuses with the egg cell to form a zygote (which develops into the embryo), while the other male gamete fuses with the two polar nuclei to form the endosperm (which provides nutrition to the developing embryo). This double fertilization is unique to flowering plants. After fertilization, the ovule develops into a seed, and the ovary develops into a fruit, protecting the seed and aiding in its dispersal."
